const faqs = [
  {question:"How do you validate a SaaS idea before building an MVP?",answer:"Define the commercial decision and riskiest assumptions, interview relevant buyers and users about current behaviour, verify the problem and buying process, test the smallest credible concept, and seek a proportionate real commitment before expanding development."},
  {question:"Do you need an MVP to validate a SaaS idea?",answer:"No. You can investigate the problem, target customer, current alternatives, buying process and access to the market before software exists. A prototype, concierge test or paid pilot may later test solution-specific assumptions with less investment than a full MVP."},
  {question:"Who should you interview for B2B SaaS validation?",answer:"Interview the people whose experience or authority affects adoption. Depending on the sale, this may include users, managers, economic buyers, technical evaluators, security or compliance specialists and procurement. Do not treat these roles as interchangeable."},
  {question:"How do you test whether customers will pay for SaaS?",answer:"Start with current spending, costs, workarounds, budget ownership and approval criteria. Then test a concrete offer and seek a realistic next commitment, such as a paid pilot, pre-order, procurement review or agreed commercial next step. A hypothetical willingness-to-pay answer is weak evidence on its own."},
  {question:"What are the strongest signs that a SaaS idea is worth pursuing?",answer:"Relevant customers repeatedly describe an important problem, take action or incur costs today, can explain a feasible buying path, see a clear reason to change and accept a proportionate next commitment. Evidence should recur in the intended segment, not only among friendly contacts."},
  {question:"When should you change or stop a SaaS idea?",answer:"Change the idea when the problem is real but the segment, workflow, value proposition, pricing, buying path or implementation assumptions are wrong. Stop when the evidence shows insufficient importance, access, differentiation or credible commitment relative to the required investment."}
];
