<main><article><header class="article-hero"><div class="container article-head"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Test Before You Build</a><span>›</span><span>Validate a SaaS idea</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">SaaS validation guide</p><h1>How to validate a SaaS idea before building an MVP</h1><p class="standfirst">Validate the expensive assumptions before turning them into software. Find out whether a defined customer has an important problem, how they solve it today, who can approve a purchase and what evidence would justify the next development step.</p><div class="byline"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="72" height="72" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen"/><div><strong>By <a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</a></strong><span>Market validation, commercialisation and customer discovery</span><time datetime={published}>Published and updated 17 September 2026</time></div></div><aside class="scope-summary"><strong>The short answer</strong><p>Define the segment and decision, interview relevant buyers and users about real past behaviour, map the buying process, test the smallest credible solution concept and ask for a proportionate commitment. Build an MVP only when it is the cheapest useful way to answer the next unresolved question.</p></aside></div></header>
<div class="container article-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#before-mvp">Before the MVP</a><a href="#assumptions">Risky assumptions</a><a href="#process">Validation process</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence strength</a><a href="#decision">BUILD, CHANGE or KILL</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></aside><div class="article-body">
<section id="before-mvp"><h2>SaaS validation should begin before the MVP</h2><p>An MVP is a way to test particular product assumptions. It is not the starting point for every SaaS idea. If you do not yet know whose problem matters, how it is handled today or who controls the purchase, building software may simply make an untested assumption more expensive.</p><p>Before writing production code, you can investigate the customer, problem, workflow, consequences, alternatives, buying process, implementation constraints and access to the market. These questions often determine whether there is a commercially plausible SaaS opportunity at all.</p><blockquote>Do not ask, “What is the smallest product we can build?” until you have asked, “What is the smallest credible test of the assumption that could stop us?”</blockquote><h3>Validation is not a request for approval</h3><p>Positive feedback, email sign-ups and feature requests can be encouraging, but none is decisive without context. Stronger evidence connects the idea to actual behaviour: repeated problems, current spending or workarounds, a reason to change, a feasible buying route and a concrete next commitment.</p></section>
<section id="assumptions"><h2>Map the assumptions that can break a SaaS business</h2><div class="card-grid"><div><h3>Customer</h3><p>Can you define and reach a group with sufficiently similar needs, context and buying conditions?</p></div><div><h3>Problem</h3><p>Does the problem occur, matter and create consequences important enough to prompt action?</p></div><div><h3>Alternative</h3><p>What software, service, spreadsheet, manual process or inaction competes with the idea?</p></div><div><h3>User and workflow</h3><p>Where would the product fit, who would use it and what behaviour or data would need to change?</p></div><div><h3>Buying process</h3><p>Who owns the budget, defines requirements, approves security and signs the agreement?</p></div><div><h3>Value and pricing</h3><p>What measurable value could justify the price, switching cost and implementation effort?</p></div><div><h3>Access</h3><p>Can you reach prospects and create a repeatable path to sales at a viable cost?</p></div><div><h3>Feasibility</h3><p>Can the promise be delivered reliably within technical, legal, data and support constraints?</p></div></div><p>Rank assumptions by uncertainty and consequence. Test the ones that could invalidate the opportunity before polishing lower-risk features.</p></section>
<aside class="inline-cta"><p class="eyebrow">Considering a SaaS investment?</p><h2>Test the market logic before committing to the MVP.</h2><p>Dr. Kari can independently recruit and interview realistic potential buyers or users, analyse the evidence and recommend whether to BUILD, CHANGE or KILL.</p><a class="button" href="/market-validation-services-pricing/">See services and pricing</a></aside>
<section id="process"><h2>A step-by-step SaaS idea validation process</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Define the decision.</strong><span>Specify the development, funding or commercial commitment the evidence must inform.</span></li><li><strong>Choose a focused initial segment.</strong><span>A narrow test is easier to interpret than feedback from unrelated markets.</span></li><li><strong>Write falsifiable assumptions.</strong><span>State what must be true about the problem, buyer, workflow, value and access.</span></li><li><strong>Recruit relevant roles.</strong><span>Include users, buyers and technical or procurement stakeholders when their evidence differs.</span></li><li><strong>Run problem interviews first.</strong><span>Explore recent events, consequences, current alternatives and decisions before presenting the idea.</span></li><li><strong>Map the B2B purchase.</strong><span>Identify triggers, budget, criteria, blockers, integrations, security and approval stages.</span></li><li><strong>Test the smallest credible concept.</strong><span>Use a workflow sketch, prototype, concierge service or landing page matched to the question.</span></li><li><strong>Seek a proportionate commitment.</strong><span>Request time, data, access, a pilot, procurement review or payment when appropriate.</span></li><li><strong>Compare evidence against criteria.</strong><span>Separate recurring patterns, segment differences, contradictions and unresolved risks.</span></li><li><strong>Choose the next action.</strong><span>BUILD, CHANGE or KILL—and state the evidence required before the following investment.</span></li></ol><p>Use the <a href="/customer-discovery-interviews/">customer discovery interview guide</a> to design the conversations and the <a href="/b2b-customer-research/">B2B participant recruitment guide</a> to reach the right people.</p></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>Match the validation method to the SaaS assumption</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Useful test</th><th>Evidence to look for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is the problem important?</td><td>Interviews and workflow observation</td><td>Recent examples, consequences, escalation and action</td></tr><tr><td>Is the segment coherent?</td><td>Compare interviews by role and company type</td><td>Recurring needs and buying conditions within the group</td></tr><tr><td>Would the solution fit?</td><td>Concept or prototype test</td><td>Comprehension, workflow fit, objections and required changes</td></tr><tr><td>Can it create value?</td><td>Concierge or manual pilot</td><td>Outcome achieved, implementation effort and repeat use</td></tr><tr><td>Will the organisation buy?</td><td>Concrete offer, paid pilot or procurement step</td><td>Budget access, approval progress and real commitment</td></tr><tr><td>Can prospects be reached?</td><td>Targeted outreach or landing-page experiment</td><td>Qualified response, meeting and conversion rates</td></tr><tr><td>Can users adopt it?</td><td>Prototype or limited product test</td><td>Task completion, continued use and switching barriers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Evidence has levels</h3><div class="comparison-grid"><div><h3>Earlier, weaker signals</h3><ul><li>interest or praise;</li><li>stated future intention;</li><li>email sign-up;</li><li>feature request;</li><li>prototype preference.</li></ul></div><div><h3>Later, stronger signals</h3><ul><li>time or data contributed;</li><li>access to decision-makers;</li><li>pilot participation;</li><li>procurement progress;</li><li>payment or signed commitment.</li></ul></div></div><p>Weaker signals are not useless. They are appropriate for early questions, but they should not be presented as equivalent to purchase evidence.</p><h3>Do not mistake a prototype for an MVP</h3><p>A clickable prototype can test comprehension and workflow without production software. A concierge test can deliver the promised outcome manually. An MVP becomes appropriate when functioning software is required to test the next important uncertainty—such as repeated use, integration or technical performance.</p></section>
<section id="decision"><h2>Decide whether to BUILD, CHANGE or KILL the SaaS idea</h2><div class="card-grid"><div><h3>BUILD</h3><p>The focused segment shows a recurring important problem, a credible reason to change, a feasible buying route and sufficient commitment to justify the next bounded investment.</p></div><div><h3>CHANGE</h3><p>The evidence supports a need, but the customer, workflow, promise, pricing, sales route, implementation model or concept must change before development continues.</p></div><div><h3>KILL</h3><p>The intended market does not show enough problem importance, access, differentiation or commitment to justify the investment, and no credible adjustment resolves the weakness.</p></div><div><h3>TEST AGAIN</h3><p>A decisive assumption remains unresolved. Define the smallest next test and avoid expanding scope until its result is known.</p></div></div><p>The recommendation should name the evidence, limitations and next threshold. It is not a guarantee of commercial success; it is a disciplined way to reduce avoidable risk before more capital and time are committed.</p></section>
